{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"42f2bf3d-a2c0-48eb-93a8-e54edc984891","agentId":"a407c19b077d521aa","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"A prior agent black-box probed the jsonschema behavior family \"object\" of the program at /workspace/dataset/test13_executable (usage: /workspace/dataset/test13_executable --a '<instance json>' --b '<schema json>'; prints validator class, True/False, top-level error count).\n\nIts findings were:\n---\nALL object keywords are no-ops when the instance is not a JSON object (array, null, string, number) — verified with a schema stacking properties+patternProperties+additionalProperties:false+required+minProperties+propertyNames against [1] and null: True/0 in every draft.\n\nPROPERTIES (all drafts, identical): each failing (property, keyword) pair yields ONE top-level error. 3 props each failing type => 3. One prop failing two keywords (type+minLength) => 2. Two props each failing two keywords => 4. Nested descent flattens: {\"o\":{\"p\":\"s\",\"q\":\"t\"}} with properties.o.properties.{p,q}:integer => 2 top-level errors; 3-level nesting with one leaf failure => 1. Properties not present in the instance are skipped. Boolean subschemas work: properties:{a:false,b:false} with both present => 2; properties:{a:true} => 0. `properties` whose value is not an object CRASHES: {\"properties\":[\"a\"]} or \"a\" => exit 1, AttributeError: 'list'/'str' object has no attribute 'items' at jsonschema/_keywords.py:294.\n\nPATTERNPROPERTIES (all drafts incl. draft3): regex is SEARCH, not fullmatch — pattern \"a\" matches key \"xay\" (1 error). A key matching N patterns is validated against ALL N subschemas and yields N separate top-level errors: {\"abc\":\"s\"} with patterns \"^a\"(integer) and \"c$\"(boolean) => 2; with 3 matching patterns all failing => 3. Boolean false subschema per matching key: {\"pa\":1,\"pb\":2} with patternProperties{\"^p\":false} => 2. A key covered by BOTH properties and patternProperties is validated by both: {\"pa\":\"s\"} with properties.pa:integer + patternProperties.\"^p\":boolean => 2. Invalid regex CRASHES at validation time: patternProperties:{\"[\":{...}} => exit 1, re.error: unterminated character set. Non-object patternProperties (a list) => exit 1, AttributeError ... 'items' at _keywords.py:20.\n\nADDITIONALPROPERTIES (all drafts incl. draft3): \"extra\" = instance keys not in `properties` and not matching any `patternProperties` regex (properties keys count as covered even if absent from the instance... more precisely, a key present in the instance and named in `properties` is not extra; properties entries absent from the instance are irrelevant). CRITICAL COUNTING ASYMMETRY: additionalProperties:false yields exactly ONE aggregate error no matter how many extras (1 extra => 1, 3 extras => 1, 5 extras => 1); additionalProperties as a SCHEMA yields ONE ERROR PER FAILING EXTRA PROPERTY (3 failing extras => 3, 5 => 5). Zero extras => 0 errors either way. Combined: {\"a\":1,\"pb\":2,\"zz\":3,\"yy\":4} with properties{a},patternProperties{\"^p\"},additionalProperties:false => 1 (both extras folded into one error); same shape with additionalProperties:{type:\"integer\"} and 2 failing extras => 2. Non-boolean, non-object additionalProperties uses PYTHON TRUTHINESS: falsy values 0, \"\", null, [] all behave exactly like `false` (1 aggregate error when extras exist, 0 when none); truthy non-object values like \"nope\" or 5 are silently ignored (0 errors, no crash). properties:{},patternProperties:{},additionalProperties:false with one key => 1.\n\nREQUIRED — array form (draft4/6/7/2019-09/2020-12 only): one top-level error PER MISSING ENTRY. 3 missing => 3, 5 missing => 5, 1 missing of 3 => 1, none missing => 0. Duplicates are NOT deduplicated: required:[\"a\",\"a\",\"b\"] with {} => 3 errors. Non-string entries are compared as-is: required:[1,2] with {} => 2 errors. required:[] => 0. A key present with value null or false satisfies required (0 errors). DRAFT3 IGNORES array-form `required` entirely at top level (True/0 even with 5 missing) — draft3 has no top-level required validator at all. Malformed forms in draft4+: required:true or required:false CRASHES (exit 1, TypeError: 'bool' object is not iterable at _keywords.py:307, raised from the is_valid call); required:5 CRASHES the same way; required:\"abc\" does NOT crash — Python iterates the string char-by-char, so {} => 3 errors (a,b,c) and {\"a\":1,\"b\":1,\"c\":1} => 0; required:{\"a\":1} iterates dict keys => 1 error. All of these malformed forms are silently ignored in draft3 (True/0).\n\nREQUIRED — draft3 boolean form inside a property subschema: {\"properties\":{\"a\":{\"required\":true},...}} yields one error per missing required property (3 missing => 3, 1 missing => 1). It uses PYTHON TRUTHINESS of the `required` value, not strict boolean: required:1 => required, required:\"x\" => required, required:[\"x\"] (non-empty array!) => required (so {\"properties\":{\"a\":{\"required\":[\"x\"]}}} with {} gives 1 error in draft3), while required:false, 0, \"\", [], {} => not required. When the property IS present, draft3 required contributes nothing and the rest of the subschema still applies ({\"a\":\"s\"} with {required:true,type:\"integer\"} => 1). In draft4+ a boolean `required` nested inside a property subschema is harmless when the property is ABSENT (subschema never runs => 0 errors), but would crash if the property were present with a bool/number required.\n\nDEPENDENCIES (draft3, draft4, draft6, draft7 ONLY — COMPLETELY REMOVED in 2019-09 and 2020-12, where it is silently ignored: every dependencies probe under d19/d20 returned True/0, including malformed dependencies:\"garbage\" and dependencies:{a:false}, which do not even crash there). Trigger key absent => no evaluation. Array-of-strings form: one error per missing dependency (3 missing => 3); non-string array elements compared as-is ([1,2] => 2 errors); empty array => 0. Two triggers each missing one dep => 2. Schema form: the dependency subschema's errors bubble up as separate TOP-LEVEL errors (2 failing props inside the dependency schema => 2; a dependency schema with required:[\"x\",\"y\"] => 2 in draft4/6/7 but 0 in draft3 since draft3 lacks array-required; a dependency schema with additionalProperties:false => 1). Boolean schema values: draft4/6/7 accept dependencies:{a:false} => 1 error and dependencies:{a:true} => 0; DRAFT3 CRASHES on both (exit 1, TypeError: 'bool' object is not iterable at _legacy_keywords.py:41 dependencies_draft3). Single-string form: ONLY draft3 supports it (dependencies:{a:\"x\"} with {\"a\":1} => 1 error, with {\"a\":1,\"x\":2} => 0); draft4/6/7 CRASH on a string dependency (exit 1, AttributeError: 'str' object has no attribute 'get' in referencing/jsonschema.py:56 _legacy_dollar_id, reached via validators.py:421 descend — i.e. they try to treat the string as a subschema). A trigger key whose value is null still triggers.\n\nDEPENDENTREQUIRED / DEPENDENTSCHEMAS (2019-09 and 2020-12 ONLY; silently ignored — True/0 — in draft3/4/6/7). dependentRequired: one error per missing entry (3 missing => 3). Its value is iterated with Python semantics, so a STRING value iterates characters: dependentRequired:{a:\"x\"} => 1 error, {a:\"xy\"} => 2 errors. dependentRequired:{a:true} CRASHES (exit 1, 'bool' object is not iterable). dependentSchemas: subschema errors bubble to top level (1 failing prop => 1; required:[\"x\",\"y\"] inside => 2); dependentSchemas:{a:false} => 1 error; dependentSchemas:{a:\"x\"} CRASHES (exit 1, AttributeError: 'str' object has no attribute 'get', referencing/jsonschema.py:50 _dollar_id). Trigger key with null value still triggers.\n\nPROPERTYNAMES (draft6+ ONLY; silently ignored in draft3 and draft4 — True/0 even when every name violates). Each (failing name, failing keyword) pair is a separate top-level error: 2 bad names with maxLength:1 => 2; 1 name failing both maxLength and pattern => 2; 2 names each failing 2 keywords => 4. propertyNames:false with 2 keys => 2 errors (one per key); propertyNames:true => 0. Empty object => 0 regardless. Names are always validated as STRINGS, so propertyNames:{type:\"integer\"} fails for every key (1 key => 1) and {\"12\":1} passes {type:\"string\"}; propertyNames:{minLength:1} rejects the empty-string key. propertyNames:\"x\" (non-schema) CRASHES in draft6+ (exit 1, AttributeError 'str' has no attribute 'get') but is ignored in draft3/4.\n\nMINPROPERTIES / MAXPROPERTIES (draft4+ ONLY; silently ignored in draft3). Exactly 1 error each when violated; both violated => 2. maxProperties:0 with {} => 0. minProperties:-1 => 0. Floats work: maxProperties:1.5 with 2 keys => 1 error; minProperties:1.0 with 1 key => 0. A string bound CRASHES in draft4+ (exit 1, TypeError: '<' not supported between instances of 'int' and 'str' at _keywords.py:313) but is ignored in draft3.\n\nUNEVALUATEDPROPERTIES (2019-09 and 2020-12 ONLY; silently ignored in draft3/4/6/7 — {x,y} with unevaluatedProperties:false => True/0 there). ALWAYS YIELDS AT MOST ONE TOP-LEVEL ERROR, aggregating all unevaluated keys — 3 unevaluated => 1, 5 unevaluated => 1. Even in SCHEMA form it collapses to 1: unevaluatedProperties:{type:\"integer\"} with 3 failing string props => 1 error (contrast additionalProperties schema form, which gives 3). With 3 props all passing the schema => 0; 1 of 3 failing => 1. unevaluatedProperties:true or {} => 0. Non-object instance => 0. Values that are neither boolean nor object CRASH: unevaluatedProperties:\"nope\" and unevaluatedProperties:0 both exit 1 (AttributeError 'str'/'int' has no attribute 'get' — it is descended into as a subschema).\n  Annotation sources that DO mark keys evaluated: `properties` (marks the key EVEN IF ITS SUBSCHEMA FAILS — {\"a\":\"s\"} with properties.a:integer + unevaluatedProperties:false => 1 error total, the type error only, proving 'a' was still annotated); `patternProperties` (real regex matching, correct); `unevaluatedProperties:true`; passing `allOf` branches; passing `anyOf`/`oneOf` branches only; `if` (annotates when the if-subschema PASSES, even with no then/else); `then`/`else` when taken; a successfully-applied `$ref`; `dependentSchemas` when triggered; and $ref together with sibling `properties` in 2019-09/2020-12 (both annotate). Nested allOf-within-allOf annotations propagate up correctly.\n  Annotation sources that DO NOT mark keys evaluated: `required`, `minProperties`/`maxProperties`, `propertyNames`, `dependentRequired`, an untriggered `dependentSchemas`, a FAILING allOf branch (allOf:[{properties:{a:{type:\"integer\"}}}] with {\"a\":\"s\"} => 2 errors: the type error plus the unevaluated error, whereas the same properties at top level gives only 1), a failing anyOf/oneOf branch (anyOf:[{properties:{a:{}}},{properties:{b:{type:\"integer\"}},required:[\"b\"]}] with {\"a\":1,\"b\":\"s\"} => 1 unevaluated error because branch 2 failed), an `if` whose subschema FAILS, a `$ref` resolving to `false`, and the subschema inside `not` (not:{properties:{a:{type:\"string\"}},required:[\"a\"]} with {\"a\":1} => 1 unevaluated error). `dependencies` cannot annotate in 2019-09/2020-12 because the keyword no longer exists there.\n  MAJOR IMPLEMENTATION BUG TO REPLICATE — additionalProperties annotation is key-based, not value-based: for `additionalProperties`, the annotation collector treats the subschema as a mapping and marks as evaluated any instance key that is literally a KEY OF THAT SUBSCHEMA OBJECT (not the properties the keyword actually applied to). Consequences, all verified: additionalProperties:true + unevaluatedProperties:false with {x,y} => 0 errors (boolean true marks every instance key), but additionalProperties:{} => 1 error, additionalProperties:{type:\"integer\"} with {\"x\":1,\"y\":2} (both passing) => 1 error, additionalProperties:{type:\"integer\"} with instance {\"type\":1} => 0 ERRORS (the key named \"type\" is \"evaluated\" because \"type\" is a key of the subschema), {\"type\":1,\"y\":2} => 1, additionalProperties:{minLength:2} with instance {\"minLength\":\"aaa\"} => 0, and additionalProperties:{type:\"integer\"} with {\"q\":1} => 1. The same key-based hack applies to a nested `unevaluatedProperties` used as an annotation source: allOf:[{unevaluatedProperties:{type:\"integer\"}}] + outer unevaluatedProperties:false gives 0 for instance {\"type\":1} but 1 for {\"q\":1}; allOf:[{unevaluatedProperties:true}] gives 0. `properties` happens to look correct under this scheme because its keys ARE property names.\n  Stacking: additionalProperties:false + unevaluatedProperties:false with 3 extras => 2 errors (1 aggregate additionalProperties + 1 aggregate unevaluated); additionalProperties:false with 1 extra + unevaluatedProperties:false => 2 in 2019/2020 vs 1 in draft3-7. A nested unevaluatedProperties inside an anyOf branch that fails produces 1 top-level anyOf error.\n\nDRAFT3 `extends` (draft3 ONLY; silently ignored in draft4/7/2020-12). Single-schema form: the extended schema's errors bubble up as separate top-level errors (1 failing prop => 1, 2 failing props => 2). Array form: errors from all elements bubble up (2 elements each failing => 2); all passing => 0; extends:[] => 0. extends combined with local keywords accumulates: extends:{properties:{a:{type:\"integer\"}}} + properties:{a:{type:\"boolean\"}} with {\"a\":\"s\"} => 2. Draft3 `required:true` inside an extends subschema works => 1. extends:true CRASHES (exit 1, TypeError: 'bool' object is not iterable at _legacy_keywords.py:88 extends_draft3).\n\nACCUMULATION SANITY CHECK: instance {\"a\":\"s\",\"x\":1,\"y\":2} against {properties:{a:{type:\"integer\"}}, additionalProperties:false, required:[\"q\",\"r\"], minProperties:9, maxProperties:1} gives 6 in draft4/6/7/2019/2020 (1 properties + 1 aggregate additionalProperties + 2 required + 1 minProperties + 1 maxProperties) and 2 in draft3 (properties + additionalProperties only, since required-array/minProperties/maxProperties do not exist there).\n\n$REF SIBLING NOTE (affects object-keyword counting): in draft4/6/7 a sibling `properties` or `required` next to `$ref` is IGNORED (=> 1 error from the ref target only), while in 2019-09/2020-12 both apply (=> 2 errors).\n\nCRASH SUMMARY (all exit code 1, full Python traceback on stderr, empty stdout — the exception is raised from the `is_valid` call at test13.py line 18, so nothing is printed at all): required:true/false/number (draft4+); dependencies:{k:\"string\"} (draft4/6/7); dependencies:{k:bool} (draft3); extends:true (draft3); dependentRequired:{k:true} and dependentSchemas:{k:\"string\"} (2019/2020); unevaluatedProperties: non-bool non-object (2019/2020); propertyNames: non-schema (draft6+); minProperties/maxProperties: string (draft4+); properties or patternProperties not an object (all drafts); invalid regex in patternProperties (all drafts).\n---\nGotchas it listed: [\"additionalProperties:false yields exactly ONE aggregate error regardless of how many extra properties (3 extras => 1 error), but additionalProperties as a SCHEMA yields ONE ERROR PER FAILING EXTRA (3 failing extras => 3 errors). Same keyword, two totally different counting rules.\",\"unevaluatedProperties ALWAYS yields at most 1 top-level error, even in schema form with 3 failing properties (=> 1, not 3). This is the opposite of additionalProperties schema form.\",\"Non-boolean, non-object additionalProperties uses Python truthiness: 0, \\\"\\\", null, and [] all behave exactly like false (aggregate error); \\\"nope\\\" and 5 are silently ignored with 0 errors.\",\"required is iterated with Python semantics and NOT deduplicated: required:[\\\"a\\\",\\\"a\\\",\\\"b\\\"] with {} => 3 errors; required:\\\"abc\\\" iterates characters => 3 errors; required:{\\\"a\\\":1} iterates dict keys => 1 error; but required:true/false/5 CRASHES with TypeError: 'bool' object is not iterable.\",\"Draft3 completely IGNORES array-form top-level `required` (0 errors even with 5 missing), and ignores minProperties/maxProperties/propertyNames/unevaluatedProperties/dependentRequired/dependentSchemas. Conversely draft4+ ignores draft3's boolean `required` inside property subschemas (when the property is absent) and ignores `extends`.\",\"Draft3 `required` inside a property subschema tests PYTHON TRUTHINESS, not strict boolean: required:[\\\"x\\\"] (a non-empty array) makes the property required, as do 1 and \\\"x\\\"; required:[] , {}, 0, \\\"\\\" do not.\",\"`dependencies` is COMPLETELY REMOVED in 2019-09 and 2020-12 — silently ignored, 0 errors, and even malformed values like dependencies:\\\"garbage\\\" or {a:false} do not crash there. Conversely dependentRequired/dependentSchemas are silently ignored in draft3/4/6/7.\",\"Single-string dependency (dependencies:{a:\\\"x\\\"}) is valid ONLY in draft3; in draft4/6/7 it CRASHES (AttributeError: 'str' object has no attribute 'get') because it is descended into as a subschema. Boolean dependency values are the mirror image: fine in draft4+, CRASH in draft3.\",\"THE BIG ONE — unevaluatedProperties annotation for `additionalProperties` is key-based, not value-based: it marks instance keys that are literally KEYS OF THE additionalProperties SUBSCHEMA OBJECT. So additionalProperties:{type:\\\"integer\\\"} + unevaluatedProperties:false marks a property named \\\"type\\\" as evaluated (instance {\\\"type\\\":1} => 0 errors) while marking normal properties unevaluated ({\\\"q\\\":1} => 1 error). additionalProperties:true marks everything; additionalProperties:{} marks nothing. The same key-hack applies to a nested `unevaluatedProperties` used as an annotation source.\",\"Top-level `properties` annotates a key for unevaluatedProperties EVEN WHEN THE SUBSCHEMA FAILS ({\\\"a\\\":\\\"s\\\"} + properties.a:integer + unevaluatedProperties:false => 1 error), but the identical `properties` inside a FAILING allOf branch does NOT annotate (=> 2 errors). Branch keywords (allOf/anyOf/oneOf/if/$ref) are validity-gated; in-place keywords are not.\",\"`if` annotates for unevaluatedProperties whenever the if-subschema passes, even with no then/else. A failing `if`, a failing $ref (to false), and the subschema inside `not` never annotate.\",\"patternProperties uses regex SEARCH (unanchored) — pattern \\\"a\\\" matches key \\\"xay\\\" — and a key matching N patterns produces N separate errors, plus additional errors if the key is also under `properties`.\",\"propertyNames is draft6+ only and produces one error per (failing key, failing keyword) pair — 2 keys each failing 2 keywords => 4 errors; propertyNames:false with 2 keys => 2 errors (not 1).\",\"Malformed schemas crash the whole program (exit 1, traceback, EMPTY stdout — no validator name line is printed) because the exception is raised inside the is_valid call. Crashers include: non-object properties/patternProperties, invalid regex, string min/maxProperties (draft4+), non-schema propertyNames (draft6+), extends:true (draft3), unevaluatedProperties as a non-bool scalar.\",\"In draft4/6/7 sibling object keywords next to $ref are ignored (1 error), while in 2019-09/2020-12 they apply alongside the ref (2 errors) — this silently changes object-keyword error counts.\",\"A key present with value null or false satisfies `required` and still triggers `dependencies`/`dependentRequired`; presence is key-membership, not truthiness.\"]\nIts vectors are in /workspace/probes/object.jsonl (903 lines of JSONL, each {\"a\",\"b\",\"out\",\"exit\"}).\n\nYou are the COMPLETENESS CRITIC. Its assignment was:\nObject keywords across drafts.\nProbe: properties (multiple failing props => error count; ORDER of iteration irrelevant but count matters), patternProperties (regex search semantics, a property matching multiple patterns => multiple errors?), additionalProperties (false => one error per extra property or one total? test 3 extra properties), additionalProperties as schema, interaction properties+patternProperties+additionalProperties.\nrequired: draft4+ array form; draft3 form where required is a BOOLEAN inside each property subschema — verify draft3 behavior and what draft3 does with an array-form required, and what draft4+ does with boolean-form required (crash/ignored). Error count when 3 required props are missing.\ndependencies (draft3-draft7: schema form and array-of-strings form and draft3 single-string form), dependentRequired + dependentSchemas (2019-09+; ignored in draft7?), and whether 'dependencies' still works in 2019-09/2020-12.\npropertyNames (draft6+), minProperties/maxProperties (draft4+; draft3?), unevaluatedProperties (2019-09+) with properties/patternProperties/additionalProperties/allOf/anyOf/if-then/$ref — probe carefully.\ndraft3 'extends' keyword (single schema and array form).\nReport exact error counts for every case.\n\nYour job: find what is MISSING or WRONG. Specifically:\n1. Read /workspace/probes/object.jsonl (use head/wc/grep, don't dump the whole thing) and check which parts of the assignment were actually probed.\n2. Identify untested edges, especially anything affecting ERROR COUNTS or cross-draft differences, and anything a JavaScript reimplementer would get wrong (unicode string length with astral chars, Python regex vs JS regex, float precision, integer vs bool, object key ordering, deep equality of numbers 1 vs 1.0, -0).\n3. RUN those missing probes yourself against the executable and APPEND them as JSONL lines to the SAME file /workspace/probes/object.jsonl (same format: {\"a\",\"b\",\"out\",\"exit\"}). Use a node script with execFileSync to append so escaping is exact. Never hand-write expected output.\n4. Verify a random sample of ~8 existing lines by re-running them and comparing to the recorded \"out\" — report any mismatch (a mismatch means the file is corrupt/hand-written).\n\nReturn: family name, MERGED and CORRECTED findings prose (restate the full spec for this family incorporating both the prior findings and everything new you learned — the implementer reads ONLY your version, so it must be self-contained and complete), the vector file path, the TOTAL line count after your appends (wc -l), and a gotchas list."},"uuid":"2c41dcfa-4f89-49e8-823d-17e463e3d157","timestamp":"2026-08-09T03:16:03.274Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-cli","cwd":"/workspace","sessionId":"fb3fcb05-14ae-40df-9980-ab8a65758794","version":"2.1.207","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
{"parentUuid":"2c41dcfa-4f89-49e8-823d-17e463e3d157","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a407c19b077d521aa","attachment":{"type":"skill_listing","content":"- deep-research: Deep research harness — fan-out web searches, fetch sources, adversarially verify claims, synthesize a cited report. - When the user wants a deep, multi-source, fact-checked research report on any topic. BEFORE invoking, check if the question is specific enough to research directly — if underspecified (e.g., \"what car to buy\" without budget/use-case/region), ask 2-3 clarifying questions to narrow scope. Then pass the refined question as args, weaving the answers in.\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- verify: Verify that a code change actually does what it's supposed to by exercising it end-to-end and observing behavior — drive the affected flow, not just tests or typecheck. Run before committing nontrivial changes; bootstraps this repo's project verify skill if none exists yet. Don't invoke it on a diff that only touches tests, docs, or other code with no runtime surface to drive (a change to product source always has one) — there's nothing to observe.\n- code-review: Review the current diff for correctness bugs and reuse/simplification/efficiency cleanups at the given effort level (low/medium: fewer, high-confidence findings; high→max: broader coverage, may include uncertain findings). Pass --comment to post findings as inline PR comments, or --fix to apply the findings to the working tree after the review.\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo, defaults to 10m) - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- init: Initialize a new CLAUDE.md file with codebase documentation\n- review: Review a GitHub pull request; for your working diff use /code-review\n- security-review: Complete a security review