ON TRUTHINESS of the `required` value, not strict boolean: required:1, \"x\", or [\"x\"] (non-empty array!) all make the property required; required:false, 0, \"\", [], {} do not. required:[\"x\"] inside a property whose key IS present => 0 (the keyword only fires on absence). When the property IS present, draft3 `required` contributes nothing and the rest of the subschema still applies ({\"a\":\"s\"} with {required:true,type:\"integer\"} => 1). draft3 `required` is honored ONLY inside `properties` subschemas — inside a patternProperties or additionalProperties subschema it does nothing for absent keys (both => 0 with instance {}). In draft4+ a boolean `required` nested inside a property subschema is a harmless no-op whether the property is ABSENT or PRESENT (verified: {\"a\":1} with properties:{a:{required:true}} => 0 in d4/d6/d7/d19/d20; required:false likewise 0) — it does NOT crash there, because it is never treated as the top-level required keyword at that position.\n\nDEPENDENCIES (draft3, draft4, draft6, draft7 ONLY — COMPLETELY REMOVED in 2019-09 and 2020-12, where it is silently ignored: every probe returns True/0, including malformed dependencies:\"garbage\" and dependencies:{a:false}, which do not even crash there). Trigger key absent => no evaluation; a trigger key whose value is null still triggers. dependencies:{} => 0. Non-object instance => 0.\n   - Array-of-strings form works in ALL of draft3/4/6/7 (draft3 included — verified 3 missing => 3 in d3, d4, d6, d7 and 0 in d19/d20). One error per missing entry; duplicates NOT deduplicated ([\"x\",\"x\"] => 2); non-string elements compared as-is ([1,2] => 2); empty array => 0; unhashable element ([{}]) CRASHES (exit 1, TypeError unhashable) in both d3 and d7. Two triggers each missing one dep => 2.\n   - Schema form: the dependency subschema's errors bubble up as separate TOP-LEVEL errors (2 failing props => 2; required:[\"x\",\"y\"] inside => 2 in d4/6/7 but 0 in d3; additionalProperties:false inside => 1