ling name, failing keyword) pair is a separate top-level error: 2 bad names with maxLength:1 => 2; 3 bad names => 3; 1 name failing both maxLength and pattern => 2; 2 names each failing 2 keywords => 4. propertyNames:false with 2 keys => 2 (one per key); propertyNames:true => 0; propertyNames:{} => 0. Empty object => 0 regardless (even propertyNames:false). Non-object instance => 0.\n   - Names are ALWAYS validated as STRINGS: propertyNames:{type:\"integer\"} fails for every key (1 key => 1); {\"12\":1} passes {type:\"string\"}; {minLength:1} rejects the empty-string key.\n   - Object keywords inside propertyNames are inert (the value is a string): {required:[\"zz\"]} => 0, {properties:{...}} => 0.\n   - const/enum work: {const:\"a\"} with {\"a\":1,\"b\":2} => 1; {enum:[\"a\",\"b\"]} with {\"a\":1,\"z\":2} => 1. `not` works ({not:{const:\"a\"}} with {\"a\":1,\"b\":2} => 1). `format` is INERT — no format checker is installed, so propertyNames:{format:\"email\"} with key \"nope\" => 0.\n   - Applicator error counting inside propertyNames: anyOf collapses to ONE error per failing key regardless of how many branches fail ({anyOf:[{maxLength:1},{const:\"z\"}]} with 2 bad keys => 2, not 4); allOf bubbles each failing branch ({allOf:[{maxLength:1},{const:\"z\"}]} with 1 bad key => 2).\n   - `pattern` inside propertyNames is also re.SEARCH: {pattern:\"^a\"} with key \"xay\" => 1 error; {pattern:\"a\"} with key \"xay\" => 0.\n   - propertyNames whose value is a non-schema scalar (\"x\") CRASHES in draft6+ (exit 1, AttributeError 'str' has no attribute 'get') but is ignored in draft3/4.\n\nMINPROPERTIES / MAXPROPERTIES (draft4+ ONLY — present in d4, d6, d7, d19, d20; silently ignored in draft3, including malformed values). Exactly 1 error each when violated; both violated => 2 (but minProperties:1 + maxProperties:0 against {} => 1, since only min is violated). maxProperties:0 with {} => 0. maxProperties:0.0 with {} => 0. minProperties:-1 => 0. Floats work and are compared numerically: maxProperties:1.5 with 2 keys => 1; minProperties:1.0 with 1 key => 0; minProperties:2.0000000000000001 parses to exactly 2.0 so 2 keys => 0 (double rounding). JSON 1e999 parses to Python inf: maxProperties:1e999 => 0 errors, minProperties:1e999 => 1 error. Arbitrarily large integer literals (10^26) work exactly (Python bigint) — maxProperties:100000000000000000000000000 => 0.\n  BOOLEANS ARE NUMBERS in Python: maxProperties:true == 1 (2 keys => 1 error); minProperties:true == 1 (1 key => 0); minProperties:false == 0 (=> 0 errors). A JS reimplementation must special-case this or booleans will be rejected/ignored.\n  CRASHES in draft4+: a string bound (exit 1, TypeError: '<' not supported between 'int' and 'str', _keywords.py:313); null (TypeError: '<' not supported between 'int' and 'NoneType'); an array bound. All ignored in draft3.\n\nUNEVALUATEDPROPERTIES (2019-09 and 2020-12 ONLY, identical in both; silently ignored in draft3/4/6/7 — {x,y} with unevaluatedProperties:false => True/0 there). ALWAYS YIELDS AT MOST ONE TOP-LEVEL ERROR, aggregating all unevaluated keys — 3 unevaluated => 1, 5 => 1. EVEN IN SCHEMA FORM it collapses to 1: unevaluatedProperties:{type:\"integer\"} with 3 failing string props => 1 (the exact opposite of additionalProperties schema form, which gives 3). With 3 props all passing => 0; 1 of 3 failing => 1. unevaluatedProperties:true or {} => 0. Empty object instance => 0. Non-object instance => 0. Values that are neither boolean nor mapping CRASH (\"nope\", 0 => exit 1, AttributeError 'str'/'int' has no attribute 'get' — descended into as a subschema).\n  ANNOTATION SOURCES THAT MARK KEYS EVALUATED: `properties` (marks the key EVEN IF ITS SUBSCHEMA FAILS — {\"a\":\"s\"} with properties.a:integer + unevaluatedProperties:false => 1 error total, the type error only); `patternProperties` by REAL regex matching, also even when the matched subschema fails ({\"pz\":\"s\"} with patternProperties{\"^p\":integer} + unevaluated:false => 1, the type error only) and correctly NOT matching a key that is merely the literal pattern text ({\"^p\":1} with patternProperties{\"^p\":{}} => 1 unevaluated error); `unevaluatedProperties:true`; passing `allOf` branches (nested allOf-in-allOf propagates up); passing `anyOf`/`oneOf` branches only; `if` when the if-subschema PASSES (even with no then/else); `then`/`else` when taken (the else branch's properties annotate, but keys only touched by the failed `if` do not); a successfully-applied `$ref`; `dependentSchemas` when triggered; and $ref together with sibling `properties` in 2019-09/2020-12 (both annotate).\n  ANNOTATION SOURCES THAT DO NOT: `required`, `minProperties`/`maxProperties`, `propertyNames` ({\"a\":1} with propertyNames:{maxLength:5} + unevaluated:false => 1), `dependentRequired`, an untriggered `dependentSchemas`, a FAILING allOf branch (allOf:[{properties:{a:{type:\"integer\"}}}] with {\"a\":\"s\"} => 2 errors vs 1 for the same properties at top level), a failing anyOf/oneOf branch, an `if` whose subschema FAILS, a `$ref` resolving to `false`, the subschema inside `not` ({\"a\":1} with not:{const:1} + unevaluated:false => 1). A `oneOf` that FAILS because BOTH branches match still annotates nothing => 1 unevaluated error. `dependencies` cannot annotate in 2019/2020 because the keyword no longer exists.\n  MAJOR IMPLEMENTATION BUG TO REPLICATE — the `additionalProperties` annotation is KEY-BASED, not value-based: the annotation collector treats the additionalProperties subschema as a mapping and marks as evaluated any instance key that is literally a KEY OF THAT SUBSCHEMA OBJECT, not the properties the keyword actually applied to. Verified consequences: additionalProperties:true + unevaluated:false with {x,y} => 0 (boolean true marks every instance key); additionalProperties:false marks NOTHING (=> extras stay unevaluated); additionalProperties:{} => 1 error (empty mapping has no keys); additionalProperties:{type:\"integer\"} with {\"x\":1,\"y\":2} both PASSING => 1 error; with instance {\"type\":1} => 0 ERRORS; with {\"type\":1,\"y\":2} => 1; with {\"type\":1,\"minLength\":2,\"q\":3} => 1 (only \"type\" is a key of the subschema); additionalProperties:{minLength:2} with {\"minLength\":\"aaa\"} => 0; additionalProperties:{properties:{}} with instance {\"properties\":1} => 0; additionalProperties:{\"__proto__\":{}} with instance {\"__proto__\":1} => 0; additionalProperties:{type:\"integer\"} with {\"q\":1} => 1. The same key-hack applies to a NESTED `unevaluatedProperties` used as an annotation source: allOf:[{unevaluatedProperties:{type:\"integer\"}}] + outer unevaluatedProperties:false gives 0 for {\"type\":1} but 1 for {\"q\":1}; allOf:[{unevaluatedProperties:true}] gives 0 for any instance. `properties` and `patternProperties` do NOT use the key-hack (properties happens to look right because its keys ARE property names; patternProperties genuinely re-matches).\n  STACKING: additionalProperties:false + unevaluatedProperties:false with 3 extras => 2 (1 aggregate + 1 aggregate); with 1 extra => 2 in d19/d20 vs 1 in d3-d7; with 0 extras (properties covers everything) => 0. required missing + unevaluated:false => 2 (1 required + 1 unevaluated). A nested unevaluatedProperties inside a FAILING anyOf branch produces 1 top-level anyOf error.\n\nDRAFT3 `extends` (draft3 ONLY; silently ignored in draft4/6/7/2019/2020). Single-schema form: the extended schema's errors bubble up as separate top-level errors (1 failing prop => 1, 2 => 2). Array form: errors from all elements bubble (2 elements each failing => 2; 3 elements each failing => 3); all passing => 0; extends:[] => 0; extends:{} => 0. extends combined with local keywords accumulates ({\"a\":\"s\"} with extends:{properties:{a:{type:\"integer\"}}} + properties:{a:{type:\"boolean\"}} => 2). Nested extends-inside-extends works => 1. Draft3 `required:true` inside an extends subschema works => 1. A draft4-style array `required` inside an extends subschema is still ignored (draft3 has no such validator) => 0. extends:{additionalProperties:false} with an extra => 1. CRASHES: extends:true, extends:false, extends:\"x\" (exit 1, TypeError: 'bool'/'str' object is not iterable, _legacy_keywords.py:88 extends_draft3). NOTE the asymmetry: extends:[true] does NOT crash and gives 0 errors (array form descends into each element as a schema, and boolean true is a valid schema).\n\n$REF SIBLING NOTE (affects object-keyword counting). In draft4/6/7 sibling object keywords next to `$ref` are IGNORED: $ref to {properties:{a:{type:\"integer\"}}} plus sibling required:[\"zz\"] with {\"a\":\"s\"} => 1 error (ref only); $ref + sibling properties/additionalProperties:false with an extra => 1. In 2019-09/2020-12 both apply: same $ref + sibling required => 2; $ref + sibling additionalProperties:false with an extra => 2; $ref + sibling properties + unevaluatedProperties:false with both props failing => 2 (both annotate, so nothing is unevaluated).\n\nUNICODE / STRING SEMANTICS (NEWLY PROBED — critical for a JS port). All string lengths are Python CODE POINT counts, not UTF-16 code units. propertyNames:{maxLength:1} against an astral key \"𝐀\" (U+1D400) PASSES (=> 0 errors) where JS .length would be 2 and wrongly fail; propertyNames:{minLength:2} against the same key FAILS (=> 1). A 2-astral-char key passes maxLength:2. A ZWJ emoji sequence (2 emoji + ZWJ = 3 code points) fails maxLength:2 => 1. Regex \".\" matches one astral code point: key \"𝐀\" matches \"^.$\" => 1 error (Python re on str is code-point based; JS needs the /u flag). Astral keys compare fine for `required` (=> 0 errors when present). NO UNICODE NORMALIZATION is performed anywhere — an NFC key U+00E9 does NOT satisfy required:[\"é\"] (NFD) => 1 error, and properties:{\"é\":{...}} never fires against an NFC \"é\" key => 0 errors; propertyNames:{maxLength:1} passes for NFC \"é\" (1 code point) but FAILS for NFD \"é\" (2 code points) => 1 error.\n\nJS-HOSTILE KEY NAMES (NEWLY PROBED). Python dicts have no prototype, so \"__proto__\", \"constructor\", \"toString\", \"hasOwnProperty\" are ordinary keys with no special behavior: {\"__proto__\":\"s\"} with properties:{\"__proto__\":{type:\"integer\"}} => 1 error in every draft; {\"__proto__\":1} with additionalProperties:false => 1; with unevaluatedProperties:false => 1; with propertyNames:{maxLength:1} => 1; with required:[\"__proto__\"] => 0 (present); required:[\"__proto__\"] against {} => 1; dependencies:{\"__proto__\":[\"z\"]} triggers (d3 and d7 => 1); dependentRequired:{\"__proto__\":[\"z\"]} triggers (d19/d20 => 1); patternProperties:{\"proto\":...} matches it => 1; {\"constructor\":1,\"toString\":2,\"hasOwnProperty\":3} with additionalProperties:false => 1 aggregate. A JS reimplementation MUST use Map / Object.create(null) / hasOwnProperty guards, and must build instances via JSON.parse (a JS object LITERAL {\"__proto__\":x} silently sets the prototype instead of an own property).\n\nOTHER KEY-LEVEL SEMANTICS. Key order never affects counts. Numeric-string keys are ordinary strings: {\"2\":\"s\",\"10\":\"t\",\"1\":\"u\"} with properties on \"1\",\"2\",\"10\" all integer => 3; required:[\"2\",\"10\"] with {\"2\":1} => 1. The empty-string key is ordinary: required:[\"\"] with {\"\":1} => 0; propertyNames:{minLength:1} with {\"\":1} => 1; additionalProperties:false with {\"\":1} and properties:{a:{}} => 1. Duplicate keys in the instance JSON text are resolved LAST-WINS by json.loads: '{\"a\":1,\"a\":\"s\"}' with properties.a:integer => 1 error (the string wins), and '{\"a\":1,\"a\":2}' counts as ONE key so minProperties:2 => 1 error.\n\nACCUMULATION SANITY CHECKS. {\"a\":\"s\",\"x\":1,\"y\":2} against {properties:{a:{type:\"integer\"}}, additionalProperties:false, required:[\"q\",\"r\"], minProperties:9, maxProperties:1} gives 6 in d4/d6/d7/d19/d20 (1 properties + 1 aggregate additionalProperties + 2 required + 1 min + 1 max) and 2 in draft3. Adding propertyNames:{maxLength:0} and unevaluatedProperties:false to that same schema gives 10 in d19/d20 (the previous 6 + 3 propertyNames, one per key + 1 aggregate unevaluated, since only \"a\" was annotated by properties), 9 in d7 (6 + 3 propertyNames, no unevaluated), and 2 in draft3. {\"ab\":\"s\",\"z\":1} against {patternProperties:{\"^a\":{type:\"integer\"},\"b$\":{type:\"boolean\"}}, additionalProperties:false} gives 3 in EVERY draft (2 pattern errors for the doubly-matched key + 1 aggregate additionalProperties for \"z\").\n\nCRASH SUMMARY (all exit 1, full Python traceback on stderr, EMPTY stdout — the exception is raised from the is_valid call at test13.py:18, so not even the validator-name line prints): properties/patternProperties whose value is not a mapping (all drafts); a properties/patternProperties ENTRY subschema that is null/string/number/array, but only when the key is present/matched; invalid Python regex in patternProperties, but only when there is at least one key to test (includes JS-style \"(?<n>...)\" named groups, \"\\p{L}\", \"[]\"); required:true/false/number/null (draft4+); required containing an unhashable element ({} or []); minProperties/maxProperties string, null, or array (draft4+); propertyNames non-schema scalar (draft6+); dependencies:{k:\"string\"} (draft4/6/7); dependencies:{k:bool} (draft3); dependencies array containing an unhashable element (draft3-7); extends:true/false/\"string\" (draft3, but NOT extends:[true]); dependentRequired:{k:true/number/null} and dependentSchemas:{k:\"string\"} (2019/2020); unevaluatedProperties as a non-bool non-mapping scalar (2019/2020).\n\nVECTOR FILE INTEGRITY: /workspace/probes/object.jsonl now has 1314 lines (903 prior + 411 appended by this review), 1210 with exit 0 and 104 with exit 1. Two independent random samples of 10 pre-existing lines each were re-executed against the binary and matched the recorded out/exit byte-for-byte (0 mismatches), so the file is machine-generated and trustworthy.","gotchas":["Missing or unrecognized $schema falls back to Draft202012Validator — full 2020-12 semantics (unevaluatedProperties active, dependencies dead). The prior report never probed this and every one of its 903 vectors carried an explicit $schema.","Python regex, not JS regex. patternProperties/propertyNames.pattern use re.search in UNICODE mode: \\d matches Arabic-Indic digits, \\w matches accented letters, `$` matches before a trailing newline (key \"a\\n\" matches \"^a$\"), \\Z is a real end anchor, inline (?i) flags work, and (?P<n>...) works while JS-style (?<n>...) CRASHES. \\p{L} and [] also crash.","Invalid regexes are compiled LAZILY per instance key: patternProperties:{\"[\":...} with instance {} is fine (0 errors), but crashes as soon as any key exists — or when additionalProperties forces the same regexes to run.","All string lengths are CODE POINTS, not UTF-16 units. propertyNames:{maxLength:1} PASSES for an astral key like U+1D400 (JS .length===2 would wrongly fail); regex \".\" matches one astral code point. And required:\"<astral char>\" iterates 1 element, not 2.","No Unicode normalization anywhere: NFC \"é\" and NFD \"é\" are different keys for required, properties, patternProperties, and propertyNames.","additionalProperties:false yields exactly ONE aggregate error regardless of extras count (3 extras => 1), but additionalProperties as a SCHEMA yields ONE ERROR PER FAILING EXTRA (3 => 3, and 2 errors if one extra fails two keywords). Same keyword, two counting rules.","additionalProperties value dispatch: ANY mapping — including the EMPTY mapping {} — is a schema (so {} means allow-all, 0 errors). Only non-mapping values fall through to Python truthiness, where 0, 0.0, -0, \"\", null, [] act like false and \"nope\", 5, 0.1, [{}] are silently ignored.","unevaluatedProperties ALWAYS collapses to at most 1 top-level error, even in schema form with 3 failing properties (=> 1, not 3) — the exact opposite of additionalProperties schema form.","THE BIG ONE — unevaluatedProperties' annotation for `additionalProperties` is KEY-BASED, not value-based: it marks instance keys that are literally KEYS OF THE additionalProperties SUBSCHEMA OBJECT. So additionalProperties:{type:\"integer\"} + unevaluatedProperties:false gives 0 errors for instance {\"type\":1} and 1 for {\"q\":1}; {\"type\":1,\"minLength\":2,\"q\":3} => 1. additionalProperties:true marks everything, additionalProperties:false and {} mark nothing. The same hack applies to a nested unevaluatedProperties used as an annotation source. patternProperties, by contrast, genuinely re-runs the regex (key \"^p\" is NOT annotated by pattern \"^p\").","required is iterated with Python semantics and never deduplicated: [\"a\",\"a\",\"b\"] => 3; \"abc\" iterates chars => 3; {\"a\":1} iterates dict keys => 1; [1.0] never matches key \"1\"; [null] is fine (1 error); but required:true/false/5/null CRASH, and required containing an unhashable element ([{}] or [[]]) ALSO CRASHES with TypeError: unhashable type — a crash mode easy to miss.","Booleans are integers in Python: maxProperties:true behaves as 1 (2 keys => 1 error), minProperties:false as 0. Also JSON 1e999 becomes inf (maxProperties:1e999 => 0 errors, minProperties:1e999 => 1), and huge integer literals are exact bigints. String/null/array bounds crash in draft4+ but are ignored in draft3.","Draft3 IGNORES top-level array-form required (0 errors even with 5 missing) and all its malformed variants, plus minProperties/maxProperties/propertyNames/unevaluatedProperties/dependentRequired/dependentSchemas. Conversely draft4+ treats a boolean `required` inside a property subschema as a harmless NO-OP whether the property is present or absent (it does NOT crash there) and ignores `extends`.","Draft3 `required` inside a property subschema uses PYTHON TRUTHINESS, not strict boolean: 1, \"x\", and even [\"x\"] (non-empty array) make it required; false, 0, \"\", [], {} do not. It only works inside `properties` — inside patternProperties or additionalProperties subschemas